  larlermorna does indeed have (some) system to the letters -- for example,
the mirroring of the voice with their unvoice equivalents.  But there have
been plenty of other lojban orthographies, too. For instance see
http://www.lojban.org/tiki/Original+lojban+orthography Or for that matter,
read about ALL the various attempts at alternate orthographies.
http://www.lojban.org/tiki/Alternate+Orthographies  Quite frankly, the
advantage of using an existing orthography that most people in the world
are already familiar with (which is used by an EXTREMELY wide range of
cultures all over the world) always far outweighs people's resistance to
having to learn an entirely new orthography, so proposals always fizzle out=
.

               --gejyspa


